public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    backtrack(nums, result, new ArrayList<>(), 0);
    return result;
}

private void backtrack(int[] nums, List<List<Integer>> result, List<Integer> current, int begin) {
    if (begin == nums.length) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
        return;
    }
    for (int i = begin; i < nums.length; i++) {
        current.add(nums[i]);
        backtrack(nums, result, current, i + 1);
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);
    }
}
```
This implementation is based on the backtracking algorithm. We use a recursive approach to generate all possible permutations by iterating over each element of the input array and recursively calling the `backtrack` method for each possible position where the element can be placed in the output list. The `backtrack` method takes four parameters: `nums` is the input array of integers, `result` is the output list of lists, `current` is the current list being constructed, and `begin` is the index of the next element to be processed. The method first checks if the `begin` index is equal to the length of the input array, which indicates that all possible permutations have been generated. If so, it adds the current list to the output list and returns. Otherwise, it iterates over each possible position where the current element can be placed in the output list, and recursively calls `backtrack` for each position. The method returns the output list of lists at the end of its execution.

